`function setup() { u = u + k; }` written before `const k = process.env.K`
and called after it puts the secret into `u`, and `fetch(u)` sends it. The
nested body is a may-run region hung off its definition (WI-faful) and
reaching definitions follow CFG order, so `k` had no edge into `setup`: the
walk saw the secret die at `console.log` and removed the flow (false clean).

cfg.late_capture_edges, run after each solve, adds an edge from every
definition placed after a nested function to each read of that name inside
it, except a read that follows the nested function's own binding of the
name. Edges only add paths: a walk can lose a False, never gain one.

